%I A214031 #36 Sep 19 2026 21:14:44
%S A214031 13,19,23,37,41,47,89,139,157,211,277,281,331,373,379,397,499,503,521,
%T A214031 571,613,619,641,647,691,733,739,743,757,761,811,853,859,863,877,983,
%U A214031 997,1051,1093,1103,1117,1171,1213,1223,1237,1289,1291,1409,1453,1459,1481,1487
%N A214031 Fixed points of A214030.
%C A214031 This sequence is to A214030 as A000057 is to A001177. It would be nice to have an interpretation of this sequence akin to the interpretation of A000057 as the set of primes which divide all Fibonacci sequences, having arbitrary initial values for a(1),a(2). The linearly recursive sequence which seems to be associated to this is 3*f(n) = 6*f(n-1) + 2*f(n-2), but this does not have integral values.
%C A214031 If we use the sequence 3,2,3,2,3,2,... instead of 2,3,2,3,... we end up with the same sequence a(n).
%C A214031 From _M. Jeremie Lafitte (Levitas)_, Sep 15 2026: (Start)
%C A214031 These are the primes p > 3 which divide at least one term of every integer sequence satisfying f(k+2) = c(k)*f(k+1) + f(k), where c(k) = 2 for even k and 3 for odd k, with arbitrary initial values f(0), f(1). Unless p divides both initial values, every p+1 consecutive terms contain exactly one multiple of p. This answers DuPre's question above.
%C A214031 Let U be the solution with U(0) = 0, U(1) = 1, and put z_U(n) = min{k >= 1: n | U(k)}. Then A214030(n) = z_U(n)-1, and the fixed-point condition is z_U(n) = n+1, which forces n to be prime.
%C A214031 Put M(c) = {{c,1},{1,0}} and B = M(3)*M(2) = {{7,3},{2,1}}. Every element p of this sequence satisfies (60/p) = -1, where trace(B)^2 - 4*det(B) = 60. With its phase recorded, the projective action on P^1(F_p) x Z/2Z consists of exactly two cycles of length p+1, each containing exactly one of the infinity states ([1:0],0) and ([1:0],1). Reversing the period from 2,3 to 3,2 exchanges the phases but gives the same primes.
%C A214031 DuPre's proposed characteristic equation 3*x^2 - 6*x - 2 = 0 gives the fixed directions of M(2)*M(3) = {{7,2},{3,1}}. Under the substitution lambda = 3*x+1, it becomes the characteristic equation lambda^2 - 8*lambda + 1 = 0, corresponding to the recurrence f(k+4) = 8*f(k+2) - f(k).
%C A214031 (End)
%F A214031 {n: A214030(n)=n}.
%F A214031 A214031 = {n >= 2: z_U(n) = ord_n(-B) = n+1}, where ord_n denotes matrix order modulo n, with U and B as defined above. - _M. Jeremie Lafitte (Levitas)_, Sep 15 2026
%e A214031 From _M. Jeremie Lafitte (Levitas)_, Sep 15 2026: (Start)
%e A214031 Let V satisfy the same alternating recurrence as U, with V(0) = V(1) = 1.
%e A214031 Modulo 23, U first returns to zero at k = 24. Thus A214030(23) = 23, so 23 belongs to this sequence. V begins 1,1,3,10,23, giving V(4) = 23. The zeros of U and V modulo 23 occur exactly at indices k == 0 (mod 24) and k == 4 (mod 24), respectively.
%e A214031 Modulo 29, (-B)^15 == I, whereas (-B)^3 == {{2,14},{19,3}} and (-B)^5 == {{15,1},{20,13}}. Hence ord_29(-B) = 15. Thus z_U(29) = 15 and A214030(29) = 14, so 29 does not belong to this sequence. The solution V never vanishes modulo 29: its residues from V(0) to V(29) are 1,1,3,10,23,21,7,13,4,25,25,13,22,21,6,10,26,1,28,27,24,12,19,11,12,18,19,17,24,2, all nonzero. Since B^15 == -I (mod 29), V(k+30) == -V(k) (mod 29); later blocks introduce no zero.
%e A214031 (End)
%o A214031 (PARI)
%o A214031 {b23(n)=local(t,m=1,s=[n]); if (n<2,0,while(1,
%o A214031 if(m%2,s=concat(s,2),s=concat(s,3));
%o A214031 t=contfracpnqn(concat(s,n));
%o A214031 t=contfrac(n*t[1,1]/t[2,1]);
%o A214031 if(t[1]<n^2||t[#t]<n^2,m++,break));m)};
%o A214031 \\ To print the sequence A214031(n) to the screen,
%o A214031 for(i=1,500,if(b23(i)==i,print1(i,", ")));
%o A214031 (PARI)
%o A214031 {is23(n)= if(n<3 || n%2==0, return(0)); my(u=1, v=0, h=(n+1)/2); for(k=1, h, v=(2*u+v)%n; if(!v, return(k==h)); u=(3*v+u)%n; if(!u, return(0)););0;};
%o A214031 for(n=1, 500, if(is23(n), print1(n, ", "))); \\ _M. Jeremie Lafitte (Levitas)_, Sep 15 2026
%Y A214031 Cf. A000057, A001177, A214030.
%K A214031 nonn,changed
%O A214031 1,1
%A A214031 _Art DuPre_, Jul 12 2012
%E A214031 a(47) corrected by _M. Jeremie Lafitte (Levitas)_, Sep 15 2026
